<ContentPage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui" xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml" xmlns:vm="clr-namespace:CafePosApp.ViewModels" xmlns:models="clr-namespace:CafePos.Core.Models;assembly=CafePos.Core" xmlns:shapes="clr-namespace:Microsoft.Maui.Controls.Shapes;assembly=Microsoft.Maui.Controls" x:Class="CafePosApp.Views.MenuPage" x:DataType="vm:MenuViewModel" Title="Меню"
             Shell.NavBarIsVisible="False">
    <ContentPage.Resources>
        <!-- One chip template for the whole strip, including the synthetic "Все" chip. It now takes
             its size and shape from the shared FilterChip style — the hand-rolled 36dp chip was under
             the 44dp touch minimum — while keeping the per-category pastel fill and the selected
             tint the menu page has always used for its visual coding. -->
        <DataTemplate x:Key="CategoryChipTemplate" x:DataType="vm:CategoryMenuItemViewModel">
            <Border Style="{StaticResource FilterChip}"
                    HeightRequest="44"
                    BackgroundColor="{Binding ChipBackground}">
                <Label Text="{Binding Name}"
                       HorizontalOptions="Center"
                       LineBreakMode="TailTruncation"
                       TextColor="{Binding ChipTextColor}"
                       FontSize="13"
                       FontAttributes="Bold"
                       VerticalTextAlignment="Center" />
                <Border.GestureRecognizers>
                    <TapGestureRecognizer Command="{Binding Source={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type ContentPage}}, Path=BindingContext.SelectCategoryCommand}" CommandParameter="{Binding .}" />
                </Border.GestureRecognizers>
            </Border>
        </DataTemplate>

        <!-- ═══ BUNDLE TILE ═══
             The price is the bundle's OWN price, not the sum of its slots. — not in the catalogue, not here. The figure is
             The sum is the à la carte reference the discount is measured against, and the discount
             is shown on the tile so the operator sees what the price means without opening the
             form. If a component's price moves and nobody re-prices the bundle, the discount line
             is where the cashier notices.

             THE DIMMED STATE AND ITS REASON come from the same "is this dish on the shelf" rule the
             sale applies, so the cashier is not led into a refusal. The sale-side resolver still has
             the last word and names the dish when it refuses.

             ═══ WHAT IS IN IT, ON THE TILE ═══
             A tile that printed only a name and a price made the operator open the composition sheet
             to answer «что входит?» — which is asked at the counter, with the customer standing there.

             IT SHAREES THE PRICE'S ROW, so the tile is no taller than it was. That is the whole trade:
             a bundle with a long composition must not size its own tile, and growing the tile would
             cost the PRODUCT GRID above it the dp it would need — the grid is what the screen is for.
             One 11pt line, already cut to a budget with an «и ещё N» tail by ComboComposition, with
             MaxLines="1" + TailTruncation on the label as the backstop below that: the common case is
             cut by a rule, the impossible case is cut by the label, and neither can wrap.

             208dp, not the 180dp it was. The strip is a horizontal ScrollView, so tile width costs
             scroll distance and nothing else, and the 28dp went to the composition column — the width
             that was actually in use. Both figures are measured on the emulator; see
             ComboComposition.TileCharBudget for the character count that goes with the width.

             NO PRICES PER DISH. The operator naming a dish needs identity, not arithmetic, and the
             bundle's own price is already on this row. Multiplicity IS printed when it is not 1,
             because «2 × Круассан» and «Круассан» are different bundles.

             And nothing here is order-specific: this row is customer-visible, and every character of
             it comes out of the catalogue. -->
        <DataTemplate x:Key="ComboTileTemplate" x:DataType="vm:MenuComboViewModel">
            <Border Style="{StaticResource CardBorderCompact}" Padding="10,8" StrokeShape="RoundRectangle 10">
                <Border.Triggers>
                    <DataTrigger TargetType="Border" Binding="{Binding IsAvailable}" Value="False">
                        <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.45" />
                    </DataTrigger>
                </Border.Triggers>
                <Border.GestureRecognizers>
                    <TapGestureRecognizer Command="{Binding Source={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type ContentPage}}, Path=BindingContext.AddComboCommand}" CommandParameter="{Binding .}" />
                </Border.GestureRecognizers>
                <VerticalStackLayout Spacing="2">
                    <Label Text="{Binding Name}" Style="{StaticResource RowTitle}" MaxLines="2" />
                    <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8">
                        <Label Text="{Binding CompositionText}" Style="{StaticResource TagText}"
                               VerticalTextAlignment="Center"
                               IsVisible="{Binding CompositionText, Converter={StaticResource IsPresent}}"
                               SemanticProperties.Description="{Binding CompositionText, StringFormat='В составе: {0}'}" />
                        <Label Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding PriceText}" FontSize="14" FontAttributes="Bold"
                               LineBreakMode="NoWrap" VerticalTextAlignment="Center" />
                    </Grid>
                    <Label Text="{Binding DiscountText}" Style="{StaticResource TagText}" FontSize="11"
                           FontAttributes="Bold"
                           TextColor="{AppThemeBinding Light={StaticResource Primary}, Dark={StaticResource PrimaryDark}}"
                           IsVisible="{Binding DiscountText, Converter={StaticResource IsPresent}}" />
                    <Label Text="{Binding UnavailableText}" Style="{StaticResource TagTextWarning}" FontSize="11"
                           IsVisible="{Binding IsAvailable, Converter={StaticResource InvertedBoolConverter}}" />
                </VerticalStackLayout>
            </Border>
        </DataTemplate>
    </ContentPage.Resources>

    <!-- ═══ ROOT ═══ -->
    <!-- Four rows: the chip strip, the bundles, the product list, the cart. The product list is the
         only `*` row, so it absorbs whatever the other three take.

         ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         WHY THERE IS NO SEARCH ON THIS PAGE
         ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         The owner's decision: «Удали поиск вообще. У меня в приложении не так много позиций, чтобы
         не найти их просто скроллом.» The catalogue is small enough that the section chips plus
         scrolling the list find anything faster than typing, and every row of chrome above the
         list pushed the two things the screen is for — the chips and the list — down.

         This page carried two searches in turn: a permanent Entry above the chips, then a magnifier
         button with a collapsible panel. Both are gone, along with the header row they lived in.
         The chip strip is now the primary way to narrow the list, and it is the first row, so it
         is the first thing under the tab bar; the product list starts higher than it ever has.
         ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

         Padding is 16 on the sides and bottom but only 8 on top. It used to be a flat 16 all
         round, which was measured on the emulator as the worst part of the header complaint:
         with the Shell nav bar gone the chips start 16dp below the status bar, and 16dp of
         empty page above a filter row reads as a gap rather than as margin. 8 is enough to
         clear the status bar inset and to keep the chip row off the system clock. The sides
         and the bottom are unchanged so the product cards and the cart keep the gutter they
         have always had. -->
    <Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto,*,Auto" Padding="16,8,16,16" RowSpacing="6">

        <!-- Single scrolling list of chips. It used to be a lone "Все" Button next to a
             CollectionView wrapped in a horizontal ScrollView, so the two scrollers competed
             for the same gesture.

             48, not 56. The 56 figure was copied from CatalogManagementPage, where the chip
             carries Margin="3,3" and so genuinely needs 50dp. THIS chip has no margin — it
             takes its 44dp straight from the FilterChip style — so a 56dp strip asked 44dp of
             chip to sit in 56dp of room and LinearItemsLayout centred the difference, putting
             6dp of nothing above the chips and 6dp more below them before the first product
             card. Two gaps where the design wants one tight band, and the lower one read as
             the row being unaligned. 48 keeps the 44dp chip whole (the clipping was the real
             defect, not the chip height) and leaves 2dp a side so the 1dp stroke is never
             shaved. The breathing room between the strip and the list is RowSpacing's job. -->
        <CollectionView ItemsSource="{Binding Categories}" SelectionMode="None" ItemTemplate="{StaticResource CategoryChipTemplate}" HeightRequest="48">
            <CollectionView.ItemsLayout>
                <LinearItemsLayout Orientation="Horizontal" ItemSpacing="6" />
            </CollectionView.ItemsLayout>
        </CollectionView>

        <!-- ═══ BUNDLES ═══
             A row of its own rather than more tiles in the product grid, because a Combo carries
             no CategoryId: folding bundles into FilteredProducts would put them under whatever
             section happened to be selected — or hide every one of them the moment a section was
             tapped. It also leaves the grid's measured column-width contract untouched:
             ProductColumnSpan sizes two product columns or one, and a third tile shape would have
             had to fight it for the same 140dp.

             The category chips do NOT filter this row — tapping «Напитки» leaves every bundle in
             place, because a bundle has no section to be missing from. The «Комбо» chip is the one
             filter that reaches it, and it works the other way round: it empties the product grid
             (IsVisible below) and leaves this row as the only content. That is what "filter to
             combos" has to mean when the two kinds live in separate collections.

             A vertical BindableLayout rather than a horizontal ScrollView, and this is the OWNER'S
             decision, superseding the earlier one that kept the tiles in a 208dp horizontal strip.

             WHAT CHANGED AND WHY. At 208dp the composition had to be cut to a character budget, and
             what the operator saw on screen was «Американо, и ещё 2» — a bundle whose contents are
             unreadable, on the one screen whose whole purpose is naming dishes to a customer. That
             is worse than a taller page. The tiles are now full width and every component is named.
             `ComboComposition.TileCharBudget` and its «и ещё N» tail are gone with it.

             The price is the bundle's OWN price, not the sum of its slots. The «по отдельности»
             reference and the discount are on the tile so the operator sees what the price means
             without opening the form, and so a component repricing shows up as a changed discount
             rather than as nothing. This row is customer-visible and every character of it comes
             from the catalogue — nothing here is order-specific. -->
        <StackLayout Grid.Row="1"
                     BindableLayout.ItemsSource="{Binding Combos}"
                     BindableLayout.ItemTemplate="{StaticResource ComboTileTemplate}"
                     Spacing="4" />

        <CollectionView Grid.Row="2" ItemsSource="{Binding FilteredProducts}" SelectionMode="None" Margin="0,0,0,2"
                        IsVisible="{Binding IsCombosOnly, Converter={StaticResource InvertedBoolConverter}}">
            <CollectionView.ItemsLayout>
                <!-- The span is set from code (see MenuPage.PublishAvailableWidth), not bound.
                     Two reasons, both measured: ItemsLayout is a BindableObject outside the visual
                     tree, so a {Binding} on it does not reliably pick up the page's BindingContext;
                     and .NET MAUI 10 has no other way to state a column width — ItemWidth was
                     removed from ItemsLayout, and a WidthRequest or MaxWidthRequest on the card is
                     ignored for sizing because the platform's ItemsWrapGrid owns the container
                     width. Dropping to a single full-width column below the threshold is the one
                     value the grid still honours, and it cannot clip. -->
                <GridItemsLayout x:Name="ProductGridLayout" Orientation="Vertical" Span="2" HorizontalItemSpacing="4" VerticalItemSpacing="4" />
            </CollectionView.ItemsLayout>
            <CollectionView.EmptyView>
                <Label Text="Ничего не найдено." Style="{StaticResource EmptyLabel}" />
            </CollectionView.EmptyView>
            <CollectionView.ItemTemplate>
                <DataTemplate x:DataType="models:Product">
                    <!-- The card takes no WidthRequest, deliberately. MAUI 10 sizes the cell itself:
                         a literal WidthRequest="150" here still measured 190px columns, and a
                         MaxWidthRequest only shrank the content. The width contract is the grid's
                         Span, set from MenuPage.PublishAvailableWidth off
                         MenuViewModel.ProductColumnSpan — see the comment on the GridItemsLayout
                         above. Both columns come from the same layout, so they are always equal. -->
                    <Border Style="{StaticResource CardBorder}" Padding="6" StrokeShape="RoundRectangle 10">
                        <Border.Triggers>
                            <DataTrigger TargetType="Border" Binding="{Binding IsAvailable}" Value="False">
                                <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.45" />
                            </DataTrigger>
                        </Border.Triggers>
                        <Border.GestureRecognizers>
                            <TapGestureRecognizer Command="{Binding Source={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type ContentPage}}, Path=BindingContext.AddProductCommand}" CommandParameter="{Binding .}" />
                        </Border.GestureRecognizers>
                        <Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto,Auto" RowSpacing="4">
                            <Label Text="{Binding Name}" Style="{StaticResource RowTitle}" MaxLines="2" />
                            <Label Grid.Row="1" Text="{Binding ., Converter={StaticResource ProductPriceText}}" FontSize="14" FontAttributes="Bold" />
                            <Label Grid.Row="2" Text="Нет в наличии" Style="{StaticResource TagText}"
                                   IsVisible="{Binding IsAvailable, Converter={StaticResource InvertedBoolConverter}}" />
                        </Grid>
                    </Border>
                </DataTemplate>
            </CollectionView.ItemTemplate>
        </CollectionView>

        <Border Grid.Row="3" Style="{StaticResource CardBorder}" Padding="6" Margin="0,0,0,0">
            <VerticalStackLayout Spacing="2">
                <!-- The restored-draft note is a CONTINUATION of the header, not a row of its own.
                     It used to sit in the message strip, where it cost a full line above the
                     checkout button to report a standing fact about the cart. See DraftNotice. -->
                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*" ColumnSpacing="5" IsVisible="{Binding HasDraftNotice}">
                    <Label Text="Корзина" FontSize="15" FontAttributes="Bold" />
                    <Label Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding DraftNotice}" Style="{StaticResource SecondaryLabel}"
                           FontSize="12" LineBreakMode="TailTruncation" VerticalTextAlignment="Center"
                           SemanticProperties.Description="{Binding DraftNotice, StringFormat='Корзина: {0}'}" />
                </Grid>
                <Label Text="Корзина" FontSize="15" FontAttributes="Bold" IsVisible="{Binding HasDraftNotice, Converter={StaticResource InvertedBoolConverter}}" />

                <!-- The cart is the one list that must stay scrollable. A fixed HeightRequest="180"
                     inside an Auto row squeezed the products list to a few cards on a short window.
                     MaximumHeightRequest bounds it proportionally: the cart takes what it needs
                     up to a cap, and the products list gets the rest.

                     MinimumHeightRequest is what makes the empty state exist at all. With only
                     a maximum, an Auto row collapses the CollectionView to zero when the cart is
                     empty, and the EmptyView had no room to render — measured on the emulator as
                     a 23px gap between "Корзина" and "Итого" with no "Пусто." anywhere, so the
                     empty cart looked identical to a cart that had failed to load. -->
                <CollectionView ItemsSource="{Binding Cart}" MinimumHeightRequest="44" MaximumHeightRequest="180" SelectionMode="None">
                    <CollectionView.EmptyView>
                        <VerticalStackLayout Padding="0,6">
                            <Label Text="Корзина пуста — выберите блюдо выше." Style="{StaticResource EmptyLabel}" />
                        </VerticalStackLayout>
                    </CollectionView.EmptyView>
                    <CollectionView.ItemTemplate>
                        <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:CartItemViewModel">
                            <!-- ═══ ONE CART LINE, ~48dp ═══
                                 Name, price and quantity on ONE row. The line total that used to sit
                                 in the third column is gone: with the quantity in a stepper two
                                 centimetres away, «220,00 ₿» beside «цена 220,00 ₽» at quantity 1 is
                                 the same number printed twice, and at quantity 3 it is the first
                                 multiplied by a figure already on screen. The cart total under the list
                                 still sums every line, so nothing is lost but the duplication.

                                 WHAT STAYS: the per-unit price, tappable. It is the number
                                 EditLinePriceCommand edits, so an editable value that is not on screen
                                 is not editable — and its "was" badge (the struck-through allowed price
                                 below it) is Bitta's, at once the deterrent while the cashier stands
                                 there and the evidence afterwards, since Price != ListPrice is exactly
                                 what the shift report's «Скидки» section reads. The badge is hidden
                                 unless something was actually overridden, so a default row is a default
                                 row.

                                 WHAT STAYS TOO: the composition, indented. A bundle that prints as one
                                 line is a bundle the kitchen cannot make and the cashier cannot check.
                                 Its tap opens the one composition sheet, pre-filled and editable, and
                                 the tap exists only for a bundle because this whole block is bound to
                                 IsVisible: on an ordinary dish it is out of the tree, so the recognizer
                                 cannot be tapped. An earlier version wrote IsEnabled="{Binding
                                 IsCombo}" on the recognizer instead — that property does not exist on
                                 TapGestureRecognizer, which is not a VisualElement, and it crashed the
                                 cart the moment the first line was added. The read of the assembly
                                 behind that: TapGestureRecognizer : GestureRecognizer : Element, whose
                                 only public members are Command, CommandParameter, NumberOfTapsRequired
                                 and Buttons. Where a control has to be conditionally inert here, it is
                                 the recognizer's Command CanExecute, never IsEnabled. -->
                            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto,Auto" ColumnSpacing="6" Padding="0,1">
                                <VerticalStackLayout Spacing="0" VerticalOptions="Center">
                                    <Label Text="{Binding ProductName}" FontAttributes="Bold" FontSize="15" LineBreakMode="TailTruncation" />
                                    <Label Text="{Binding SelectedVariantName, StringFormat='[{0}]'}" Style="{StaticResource SecondaryLabel}" FontSize="11" LineBreakMode="TailTruncation" IsVisible="{Binding SelectedVariantName, Converter={StaticResource IsPresent}}" />
                                    <Label Text="{Binding SelectedModifierName, StringFormat='({0})'}" Style="{StaticResource SecondaryLabel}" FontSize="11" LineBreakMode="TailTruncation" IsVisible="{Binding SelectedModifierName, Converter={StaticResource IsPresent}}" />

                                    <VerticalStackLayout Spacing="0" Padding="12,1,0,0"
                                                         IsVisible="{Binding IsCombo}">
                                        <VerticalStackLayout.GestureRecognizers>
                                            <TapGestureRecognizer
                                                                  Command="{Binding Source={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type ContentPage}}, Path=BindingContext.EditLineCompositionCommand}"
                                                                  CommandParameter="{Binding .}" />
                                        </VerticalStackLayout.GestureRecognizers>
                                        <VerticalStackLayout BindableLayout.ItemsSource="{Binding Components}" Spacing="0">
                                            <BindableLayout.ItemTemplate>
                                                <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:LineComponentViewModel">
                                                    <Label Text="{Binding Text}" Style="{StaticResource TagText}" FontSize="11" LineBreakMode="TailTruncation" />
                                                </DataTemplate>
                                            </BindableLayout.ItemTemplate>
                                        </VerticalStackLayout>
                                    </VerticalStackLayout>
                                </VerticalStackLayout>

                                <!-- The charged unit price, and under it — only when something was
                                     actually overridden — the price it was allowed to be. -->
                                <VerticalStackLayout Grid.Column="1" Spacing="0" VerticalOptions="Center">
                                    <Label Text="{Binding PriceText}" FontAttributes="Bold" FontSize="15"
                                           HorizontalTextAlignment="End" LineBreakMode="NoWrap"
                                           SemanticProperties.Description="{Binding PriceText, StringFormat='Цена {0}. Нажмите, чтобы изменить.'}">
                                        <Label.GestureRecognizers>
                                            <TapGestureRecognizer Command="{Binding Source={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type ContentPage}}, Path=BindingContext.EditLinePriceCommand}"
                                                                  CommandParameter="{Binding .}" />
                                        </Label.GestureRecognizers>
                                    </Label>
                                    <Label Text="{Binding ListPriceText}" Style="{StaticResource TagText}" FontSize="11"
                                           TextDecorations="Strikethrough" HorizontalTextAlignment="End"
                                           LineBreakMode="NoWrap"
                                           IsVisible="{Binding IsPriceOverridden}"
                                           SemanticProperties.Description="Разрешённая цена" />
                                </VerticalStackLayout>

                                <!-- ═══ THE STEPPER IS 40dp, AND THAT IS THE OWNER'S DECISION ═══
                                     The 48dp argument was made and OVERRULED. Recorded here so the next
                                     reader sees a decision rather than an oversight, and sees what it cost.

                                     48dp at wm density 420 is 126px — 7.6 mm of glass, under the 9–10 mm
                                     published for a 7–11″ tablet. 40dp is 105px, 6.3 mm. So this control
                                     is now further below that floor than it was. The stated reason was
                                     that two steppers flanking the quantity spent 96dp of a 411dp row on
                                     two glyphs while the dish name and price — the things actually read
                                     — were compressed to fit. The name and price are now 15sp.

                                     .opencode-rules.md §6 states the floor in MILLIMETRES precisely
                                     because a dp threshold means a different physical size on every
                                     screen and therefore lies silently; this row is now a named
                                     exception in that document rather than a silent violation. The
                                     remaining way to buy space is DELETING ROWS — that is where it
                                     came from before — not shrinking targets. Anyone revisiting this
                                     should shrink rows, not buttons.

                                     40dp of button beside a 40dp quantity column IS the line, so the
                                     text has to fit inside it. The buttons are tonal and the number is
                                     19sp bold: solid Primary around a small digit was measured as the
                                     quantity reading as a caption on its own controls.

                                     BUTTONS, NOT A TEXT FIELD. Baymard found 61% of sites get the quantity
                                     control wrong and, of the redesigns, 97% — dropdowns and text inputs
                                     producing literal failures like a quantity of "21" instead of "2". -->
                                <Grid Grid.Column="2" ColumnDefinitions="40,40,40" ColumnSpacing="0" VerticalOptions="Center">
                                    <Button Text="−" Style="{StaticResource StepperButton}" SemanticProperties.Description="Убрать одну штуку" Command="{Binding Source={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type ContentPage}}, Path=BindingContext.RemoveItemCommand}" CommandParameter="{Binding .}" />
                                    <Label Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding Quantity}" FontSize="19" FontAttributes="Bold" WidthRequest="40" HorizontalTextAlignment="Center" VerticalTextAlignment="Center" SemanticProperties.Description="Количество" />
                                    <Button Grid.Column="2" Text="+" Style="{StaticResource StepperButton}" SemanticProperties.Description="Добавить ещё одну" Command="{Binding Source={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type ContentPage}}, Path=BindingContext.AddItemCommand}" CommandParameter="{Binding .}" />
                                </Grid>
                            </Grid>
                        </DataTemplate>
                    </CollectionView.ItemTemplate>
                </CollectionView>

                <!-- The cart's top edge. It used to sit directly above a separate «Итого» row; that row
                     is gone and the rule still divides what it divided — the dish lines from the
                     footer. The total itself moved INTO the checkout button below («Оплатить 220.00 ₽»),
                     which is the owner's choice over printing it beside the button: both cost the same
                     row, and a button naming what it charges leaves one focus point instead of a
                     figure and a verb to associate before pressing anything. Baymard's 2024 study
                     recorded a tester asking of a checkout button, verbatim, "I'm not sure if I click
                     the 'Next' button, will it charge?" — this is the answer to that, on the control
                     itself. See MenuViewModel.PayAndCreateText. -->
                <BoxView HeightRequest="1" Color="{AppThemeBinding Light={StaticResource Gray200}, Dark={StaticResource Gray600}}" />

                <!-- ═══ FULFILMENT, CONTACT AND TIME — ONE ROW, EXPANDING IN PLACE ═══
                     Three facts about the ORDER rather than about any line: a customer either takes
                     the whole thing away or eats all of it on the premises, so they are asked once,
                     here, and never per line.

                     They are no longer always visible. Measured on the emulator, this block cost
                     402px — 153dp — of the cart's 904px, on a cart that was TALLER than the product
                     grid it sits under (666px of grid, 340px of unused slack in the row above it).
                     The chrome was costing more than the content, and the three things in it are the
                     three things an operator touches least: the owner has confirmed they are used
                     rarely, and frequency of use is the rule for what belongs on the first level.
                     The grounding is written out at length on MenuViewModel.IsFulfilmentExpanded —
                     NN/g's hotel-reservation segment, the two-disclosure-levels ceiling, and
                     Chimera et al. 1994 on expand/contract beating a stable interface.

                     THE ROW STATES THE CURRENT STATE WHILE COLLAPSED, so nothing is hidden BY the
                     collapse: it reads «В зале · готово сейчас» and the operator can see the two
                     decisions are right without opening anything. What the collapse removes is the
                     CONTROLS, not the facts.

                     WHILE EXPANDED IT SAYS SOMETHING ELSE, and this is the second half of the change.
                     Open, the row reads only «Параметры выдачи»: directly beneath it sit the control
                     that changes fulfilment and the «Когда · Готово сейчас» line saying the same thing,
                     so repeating the state would print each fact twice inside three rows — and the row
                     would then contradict the button under it, since a row reading «В зале» above a
                     button reading «Сделать с собой» is two statements of one fact. Both captions are
                     computed from ONE property off the same state, so the row and the controls beneath
                     it cannot drift apart. See MenuViewModel.FulfilmentSummary, which is the whole
                     argument; the visible text is one computed value, not two strings kept in step.

                     THE ROW IS THE TARGET, IN FULL, and it is 48dp tall — not a small chevron at the
                     end of a line. An expanding row sized to its arrow is a mis-tap waiting to
                     happen, and the fix for that is not a smaller arrow but a bigger target. Nothing
                     else in the cart Border is inside this Border, so a tap on a dish line or on a
                     checkout button cannot reach it.

                     Hidden while the cart is empty. Three questions with nothing to sell under them
                     invite answers that then have to be thrown away with the cart — and a phone typed
                     against an empty cart is the worst version of that, because it would survive into
                     the NEXT order and be written to it by a checkout nobody chose it for. -->
                <VerticalStackLayout Spacing="4" IsVisible="{Binding CanCreateOrder}">

                    <Border MinimumHeightRequest="48" Padding="8,2" Margin="0,4,0,0"
                            StrokeShape="RoundRectangle 8"
                            BackgroundColor="{AppThemeBinding Light={StaticResource SurfaceVariant}, Dark={StaticResource SurfaceVariantDark}}">
                        <Border.GestureRecognizers>
                            <!-- On the ROW Border, and only on it: the expanded block below is a SIBLING
                                 of this Border, not a child, so a tap inside it never toggles. That is
                                 what makes typing a phone into the expanded block possible at all. -->
                            <TapGestureRecognizer Command="{Binding Source={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type ContentPage}}, Path=BindingContext.ToggleFulfilmentCommand}" />
                        </Border.GestureRecognizers>
                        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8">
                            <Label Grid.Column="0" Text="{Binding FulfilmentSummary}" FontSize="14" FontAttributes="Bold"
                                   VerticalOptions="Center" LineBreakMode="TailTruncation"
                                   SemanticProperties.Description="{Binding FulfilmentToggleHint}" />
                            <!-- A chevron, not a glyph. «+»/«−» is the stepper's vocabulary and would read
                                 as add/remove; and a text character is a font-fallback risk — the
                                 implicit style sets FontFamily=OpenSansRegular, which has no glyph for
                                 «⌄». Data is a LITERAL Geometry in both triggers and only the Data
                                 changes, so the AppThemeBinding-in-a-Setter trap is not in play: the
                                 stroke colour is set once, as an attribute, where it follows the theme. -->
                            <shapes:Path Grid.Column="1" Aspect="Uniform" WidthRequest="16" HeightRequest="16"
                                         VerticalOptions="Center" HorizontalOptions="End"
                                         Stroke="{AppThemeBinding Light={StaticResource Gray700}, Dark={StaticResource Gray300}}"
                                         StrokeThickness="2" Fill="Transparent"
                                         Data="M4,2 L14,8 L4,14">
                                <shapes:Path.Triggers>
                                    <DataTrigger TargetType="shapes:Path" Binding="{Binding IsFulfilmentExpanded}" Value="True">
                                        <Setter Property="Data" Value="M2,5 L8,11 M14,5 L8,11" />
                                    </DataTrigger>
                                </shapes:Path.Triggers>
                            </shapes:Path>
                        </Grid>
                    </Border>

                    <VerticalStackLayout Spacing="4" IsVisible="{Binding IsFulfilmentExpanded}">
                        <!-- THE CONTROL THAT DECIDES WHETHER A CONTACT FIELD EXISTS — and it is ONE
                             button, not a two-segment toggle.

                             Counter service is the default and it stays the default: the phone row below
                             appears and disappears with this. A phone kept for a customer eating on the
                             premises is personal data with no purpose behind it, which is what 152-ФЗ
                             ст. 5(4)-(5) is about, and CheckoutService drops the value at the service
                             boundary in any case.

                             WHY ONE BUTTON STATING THE CHANGE. «В зале» / «С собой» as two segments
                             restated what the collapsed row above already says, and while this block is
                             open that row reads «Параметры выдачи» — so the segments were the only place
                             the state appeared, which is precisely the duplication this design set out
                             to delete. Worse, a filled segment labelled «В зале» directly above a button
                             labelled «Сделать с собой» asks the reader to reconcile two statements of one
                             fact. «Сделать с собой» / «Сделать в зале» names what the tap DOES and needs
                             no second segment to be unambiguous, which is also one fewer thing to
                             mis-tap on a screen whose numbers are money. The button is at 48dp like
                             every other control here: the rows were deleted, the targets were not
                             shrunk. See MenuViewModel.ToggleOrderTypeText.

                             The current state is still reachable without a second control: the
                             collapsed row states it, the caption names the opposite, the phone row
                             appears only for takeaway, and ToggleOrderTypeHint says both out loud for a
                             screen reader — because the visible caption is deliberately the second and
                             never the first.

                             NO IsEnabled HERE AND ON NO TapGestureRecognizer ANYWHERE: it is not a
                             VisualElement and has no such property — the XamlParseException that threw
                             is written out on the cart line's composition block above. Where a control
                             has to be conditionally inert here it is Command.CanExecute, plus
                             NotifyCanExecuteChanged(). -->
                        <Button Text="{Binding ToggleOrderTypeText}" HeightRequest="48" MinimumHeightRequest="48"
                                FontAttributes="Bold" Padding="0"
                                Command="{Binding ToggleOrderTypeCommand}"
                                SemanticProperties.Description="{Binding ToggleOrderTypeHint}" />

                        <!-- The phone, TAKAWAY ONLY. Hidden outright for counter service rather than
                             disabled: a greyed-out field still invites a tap and still invites a question,
                             and the whole point of 152-ФЗ ст. 6(1)(5) here is that the question is never
                             asked. -->
                        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="6" Margin="0,2,0,0" IsVisible="{Binding IsTakeaway}">
                            <VerticalStackLayout Spacing="0" VerticalOptions="Center">
                                <Label Text="{Binding PhoneDisplayText, StringFormat='Телефон: {0}'}" Style="{StaticResource SecondaryLabel}"
                                       IsVisible="{Binding HasCustomerPhone}" />
                                <Label Text="Телефон не указан" Style="{StaticResource EmptyLabel}" Padding="0"
                                       IsVisible="{Binding HasNoCustomerPhone}" />
                            </VerticalStackLayout>
                            <Button Grid.Column="1" Text="Указать телефон" Style="{StaticResource SecondaryButton}"
                                    MinimumHeightRequest="44" Command="{Binding EditPhoneCommand}"
                                    SemanticProperties.Description="Ввести телефон клиента и проверить его вместе с покупателем" />
                        </Grid>

                        <!-- «Когда». ONE control reading «сейчас» by default — not a checkbox beside a
                             field. An absent time IS as soon as possible (Order.RequestedAt == null) and
                             the promise is then the lead time, so a second "ASAP" flag would store the
                             same absence twice and the two copies could disagree. Choosing a clock time
                             switches it; that is the whole interaction.

                             AND A TIME THAT HAS ALREADY PASSED IS MARKED, NOT REFUSED. The second line
                             appears only then, so a normal row costs nothing; an operator who has just
                             promised a customer a moment that has gone is told so on the row they are
                             looking at, and the order still books overdue rather than being silently
                             pushed to tomorrow. See MenuViewModel.IsRequestedTimeLate. -->
                        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="6" Margin="0,2,0,0">
                            <Label Grid.Column="0" Text="Когда" Style="{StaticResource FieldLabel}" Margin="0,0,0,0" VerticalOptions="Center" />
                            <VerticalStackLayout Grid.Column="1" Spacing="0" VerticalOptions="Center">
                                <Label Text="{Binding RequestedTimeText, StringFormat='Готово {0}'}" FontSize="14" FontAttributes="Bold" />
                                <Label Text="Время уже прошло" Style="{StaticResource TagTextWarning}" FontSize="11"
                                       IsVisible="{Binding IsRequestedTimeLate}" />
                            </VerticalStackLayout>
                            <Button Grid.Column="2" Text="Выбрать время" Style="{StaticResource SecondaryButton}" MinimumHeightRequest="44"
                                    Command="{Binding PickTimeCommand}"
                                    SemanticProperties.Description="Выбрать, когда приготовить заказ" />
                        </Grid>

                        <!-- WHAT THE CUSTOMER IS TOLD, IN WORDS, and it lives HERE rather than on its own
                             permanent line. It is prose competing with controls for a row, and it only
                             earns its space while somebody is actually setting the fulfilment mode —
                             which is the same frequency argument that collapsed this block.
                             A RANGE, never a clock time derived from the lead time: Management Science
                             71(9) 2025, eight preregistered experiments, N=5323 — a range is rated better
                             than a point estimate even when the point estimate equals the range's upper
                             bound, and an extremely wide range is rated WORSE than a point. The bounds,
                             and the reason they are only two minutes wide, live in
                             MenuViewModel.PromiseText.

                             A named time does print as a clock time, and that is not the same claim: it is
                             what the customer asked for, repeated back so they can correct it. Quoting an
                             estimate for an agreed appointment would only obscure what was agreed. -->
                        <Label Text="{Binding PromiseText}" FontSize="13" FontAttributes="Bold" Margin="0,2,0,0"
                               TextColor="{AppThemeBinding Light={StaticResource Tertiary}, Dark={StaticResource PrimaryDark}}" />
                    </VerticalStackLayout>
                </VerticalStackLayout>

                <!-- The message sits above the button it reports on, not below it. "Заказ #1
                     создан на 640.00 ₽" is the most important feedback in the app and it was
                     the last line on the screen, in SecondaryLabel grey, styled identically to
                     a failure. Now it is bold, above the action, and coloured by outcome —
                     colour reinforcing the text, never replacing it.

                     The colour is a BINDING, and that is the whole point. It used to be two
                     DataTriggers setting TextColor to a bare {StaticResource Danger} and
                     {StaticResource Success}. A trigger's Setter takes a value, not a binding
                     expression, so each token was resolved once at parse time against the light
                     theme and could never follow a theme change: measured on a Pixel 7 in dark
                     theme this label painted Success #2E7D32 on #1E1E1E, 3.28:1, where
                     SuccessDark would have been 8.28:1. MenuViewModel.MessageColor resolves
                     against the live theme instead; see its remarks for the measurement.

                     «Отменить» RIDES IN THIS ROW rather than getting one of its own, so an undo
                     costs no additional row: the strip is there whenever a message is, and absent
                     otherwise. It is offered for exactly the removal that armed it — Message's setter
                     retires it — so it can never sit beside an unrelated outcome. See
                     MenuViewModel.HasUndo. -->
                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8" Margin="0,2,0,0" IsVisible="{Binding HasMessage}">
                    <Label Text="{Binding Message}" FontSize="14" FontAttributes="Bold" VerticalOptions="Center"
                           LineBreakMode="WordWrap" TextColor="{Binding MessageColor}" />
                    <Button Grid.Column="1" Text="Отменить" Style="{StaticResource SecondaryButton}"
                            MinimumHeightRequest="44" Padding="8,4" FontSize="13"
                            IsVisible="{Binding HasUndo}" Command="{Binding UndoRemoveCommand}"
                            SemanticProperties.Description="Вернуть позицию, которую только что убрали из корзины" />
                </Grid>

                <!-- ═══ CHECKOUT: ONE BUTTON, AND IT NAMES THE AMOUNT ═══
                     «Оплатить 220.00 ₽» — the action and the figure in one control, full width.

                     The figure is inside the button rather than beside it. That is the owner's explicit
                     choice over putting the total in a row to the left: both save the same row, and
                     this leaves ONE focus point instead of a number and a verb the operator has to
                     associate before pressing anything. A button that names the amount it charges also
                     answers the question Baymard's 2024 study recorded a tester asking verbatim — "I'm
                     not sure if I click the 'Next' button, will it charge?" — on the control itself
                     rather than in a line above it.

                     «Оплата при выдаче» IS NOT HERE. It was the second half of this pair, and it is now
                     the second exit from the PAYMENT SHEET: it is a decision about when the money
                     moves, so it lives at the moment the money moves, where the sheet can ask it as the
                     counterpart to «Принять оплату». Two buttons became one, and the sheet is the only
                     place a fork is asked for. The sheet only offers it on THIS path — the board's
                     top-up and the details page's «Принять оплату» open the same sheet for an order that
                     already exists, so there is nothing there to create and nothing to defer.

                     48dp, FILL WIDTH, unshrunk. The rows above it are what the compacting deleted; no
                     control on this page got smaller to pay for it. See the cart line's comment for why
                     the stepper's 48dp is the floor of this block rather than its target. -->
                <Button Text="{Binding PayAndCreateText}" HeightRequest="48" MinimumHeightRequest="48"
                        FontAttributes="Bold" Padding="0"
                        IsEnabled="{Binding CanCreateOrder}" Command="{Binding PayAndCreateCommand}"
                        SemanticProperties.Description="{Binding PayAndCreateText, StringFormat='{0}. Создать заказ и принять оплату сейчас.'}" />
            </VerticalStackLayout>
        </Border>
    </Grid>
</ContentPage>
